#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483488} :activity/id #uuid "13de39d3-3bb8-598c-9ef9-38c2d90d7a15" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049459 :topic/id #uuid "f6fd4776-56f0-5112-9cdc-83e344004d93" :topic/math-academy-id 2131} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483433 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483395 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14e8ce4b-4397-5131-8016-ac24d8139492" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482848} {:answer/id #uuid "4c0cb281-c41d-56fc-b840-37c1e1e299b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482947} {:answer/id #uuid "86a9b14f-682a-56ff-b5d7-630ab6d0e48f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483046} {:answer/id #uuid "8c82e88d-36a4-5cf5-b6fa-804eb0316710" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483060} {:answer/id #uuid "d8ee5433-5dac-5d77-ba1c-0052b43bbfb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483177}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483060} :answer-field/id #uuid "fb66c9a3-57be-589b-8044-dbc471e3b934" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483341}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "aefd685f-6837-5195-a54c-56ec619b88d6" :question/math-academy-id "q-105574" :question/problem "![](images/c1/c1dd3e659d2580103232fa68620c60f493477649e1c4a8880023e6c6de88a167.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true everywhere in the domain of $F?$\n\nI. $∇\\cdot F=0$\n\nII. $∇\\cdot F>0$\n\nIII. $∇\\cdot F<0$\n\nIV. $F$ is solenoidal" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{div}\\,F=∇\\cdot F$ denotes the divergence of the vector field $F.$\n\nIntuitively, the divergence of $F$ at a point $P$ measures how much $F$ acts like a source or a sink at $P:$\n\n- If $F$ acts like a source at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F>0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $F$ acts like a sink at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F<0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $F$ acts like neither a source nor a sink at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F=0$ at $P.$\n\n![](images/6c/6c747149618d4c5ef35e727af15cd001365bd9c2d5e064689baf24c6ee23cff9.png)\n\nIf $∇\\cdot F=0$ everywhere in the domain of $F,$ we say that $F$ is solenoidal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statement III is true, whereas statements I and II are false. Notice that the lengths of the vector's $y$-components decrease as we move vertically towards the origin. Therefore, the vector field $F$ acts like a sink at every point.\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since $∇\\cdot F\\ne 0,$ the vector field $F$ is not solenoidal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "05100288-b9ed-59fd-b7a9-9a2592fbbb50" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483450}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483443 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266056 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10bcdd62-0b56-5da2-a50a-586faa6696b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "xy{z}^{2}+xz" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263155} {:answer/id #uuid "295ca0f4-57e7-5a42-ac1c-c9565fd958a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y{z}^{2}+xy{e}^{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263355} {:answer/id #uuid "2be15acb-dbc4-5b00-98a8-38e26089e1ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5y{z}^{2}+x{e}^{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263371} {:answer/id #uuid "40fe9bd6-26ef-51ba-a8ee-4aa3a8d37085" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y{z}^{2}+xy+y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263576} {:answer/id #uuid "fe783955-ec8b-51ba-88fd-5f0cc372a593" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x+{e}^{y}+{z}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265240}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186263371} :answer-field/id #uuid "6647713f-61bd-5d5d-9904-3d926c79511c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265424}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "01f7efc1-008b-5b39-b852-37db53b31085" :question/math-academy-id "q-131176" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F=2xy{z}^{2}\\,i+x{e}^{y}\\,j+y{z}^{3}\\,k.$ \nFind $∇\\cdot F.$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j+R\\,k$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the divergence of $F$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n∇\\cdot F=\\text{div}\\,F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot F & =\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2xy{z}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{e}^{y})+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(y{z}^{3}) \\\\  & =2y{z}^{2}+x{e}^{y}+3y{z}^{2} \\\\  & =5y{z}^{2}+x{e}^{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "1b54e950-07e6-508f-8bbf-26f7181e85f0"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483450 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259490 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "71cbe9b9-abfb-59ee-9a3b-f7f689c26688" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "144" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257781} {:answer/id #uuid "88b7f1be-ad85-5371-a6af-84faa05bd3aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "76" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257981} {:answer/id #uuid "d1c60dec-38f0-52c7-b0aa-6a4369234d40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "48" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258609} {:answer/id #uuid "d40a47e0-a307-5498-8db8-91105ba44fa3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "96" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258639} {:answer/id #uuid "edb9593d-e1cc-5ea7-ae77-b57065d0d67e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "84" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258838}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258639} :answer-field/id #uuid "31c01b0b-506b-5b5b-95a1-7bc8278302dd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259120}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "74e7bf88-8da6-528c-84d5-ded22deb1ee4" :question/math-academy-id "q-123200" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F=(x-y)^{3}\\,i+(y+z)^{3}\\,j+(x+z)^{3}\\,k.$ Evaluate $\\text{div}\\,F$ at the point $(-2,2,2).$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j+R\\,k$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the divergence of $F$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n\\text{div}\\,F=∇\\cdot F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot F & =\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}((x-y)^{3})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}((y+z)^{3})+\\frac{∂}{∂z}((x+z)^{3}) \\\\  & =3(x-y)^{2}+3(y+z)^{2}+3(x+z)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we evaluate $\\text{div}\\,F$ at the point $(-2,2,2),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F(-2,2,2) & =3((-2)-(2))^{2}+3((2)+(2))^{2}+3((-2)+(2))^{2} \\\\  & =3(-4)^{2}+3(4)^{2}+3(0)^{2} \\\\  & =96.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "2e0d18ce-acf0-5440-b4c0-fe1c45eb2401" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483443}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483483 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483360 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "43bc396a-d9ed-5bb3-b93d-0fb796ec2097" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482927} {:answer/id #uuid "516c5078-1161-5f0b-8b9d-0636463d00b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482956} {:answer/id #uuid "7b6cb7f0-12ce-523b-a991-3f45078b3156" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483028} {:answer/id #uuid "87d47221-a635-52a3-99d0-f5a56e12e0ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483050} {:answer/id #uuid "94b6c747-e673-5cf6-a43f-ad3df20d8406" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483071}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483028} :answer-field/id #uuid "22420a9a-1575-5698-aa34-04729efa2a61" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483249}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "3aad6c37-ff96-5a6c-89b9-a9f32a30b3d7" :question/math-academy-id "q-105570" :question/problem "![](images/2b/2b64c156519b685acf390c51c1ea3e1540d82e0f7e047cf2f804d38a23956604.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true everywhere in the domain of $F?$\n\nI. $∇\\cdot F=0$\n\nII. $∇\\cdot F>0$\n\nIII. $∇\\cdot F<0$\n\nIV. $F$ is solenoidal" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{div}\\,F=∇\\cdot F$ denotes the divergence of the vector field $F.$\n\nIntuitively, the divergence of $F$ at a point $P$ measures how much $F$ acts like a source or a sink at $P:$\n\n- If $F$ acts like a source at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F>0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $F$ acts like a sink at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F<0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $F$ acts like neither a source nor a sink at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F=0$ at $P.$\n\n![](images/6c/6c747149618d4c5ef35e727af15cd001365bd9c2d5e064689baf24c6ee23cff9.png)\n\nIf $∇\\cdot F=0$ everywhere in the domain of $F,$ we say that $F$ is solenoidal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statement II is true, whereas statements I and III are false. Notice that the length of the vectors' $x$-components increase as we move away from the origin in the horizontal direction. Therefore, the vector field $F$ acts like a source at every point.\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since $∇\\cdot F\\ne 0,$ the vector field $F$ is not solenoidal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} :step/id #uuid "9e3f811c-54a6-5c26-bdfa-8aaa897c63e3" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483433}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483488 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483377 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4a8a0905-3f5a-57f9-b71d-c2127323b463" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2-\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482943} {:answer/id #uuid "5b08ebea-7284-5412-99de-e04b22ab6d40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4+\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482971} {:answer/id #uuid "5db37158-5959-5928-a4a3-8612494e2e92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482973} {:answer/id #uuid "7a1781db-53be-5369-8346-96df148295ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483025} {:answer/id #uuid "d185a6d1-cc28-52e1-8c76-58d00373669d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3+\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483162}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483162} :answer-field/id #uuid "346d1a8e-e297-5ff1-8dff-7d5740531d20" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483254}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "75ca7d55-6e3f-58c8-8a50-805ce5d1a756" :question/math-academy-id "q-41838" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F={x}^{2}yz\\,i+{x}^{3}y\\,j+z\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{y}{x})\\,k.$ Evaluate $\\text{div}\\,F$ at the point $(-1,-1,2).$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j+R\\,k$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the divergence of $F$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n\\text{div}\\,F=∇\\cdot F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot F & =\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}yz)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{3}y)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(z\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{y}{x})) \\\\  & =2xyz+{x}^{3}+\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{y}{x})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate $\\text{div}\\,F$ at the point $(-1,-1,2),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F(-1,-1,2) & =2(-1)(-1)(2)+(-1)^{3}+\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{-1}{-1}) \\\\  & =4-1+\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =3+\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "adea8609-2822-5adc-b454-084b01390c94" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483483}}] :activity/title "The Divergence of a Vector Field" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482799}]}
